  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, in a statement carried by the Washington Times and Spokesman-Review (Aug 9), named four explicit conditions for Iran to return to direct US negotiations: full lifting of the US naval blockade, withdrawal of US regional military forces, removal of all US sanctions, and payment of war-damage compensation. Araghchi limited ongoing US-Iran contact to written messages relayed through Pakistani and Qatari intermediaries — a channel the resolution criteria already codes as AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO. None of the four conditions were approached or addressed in any public US statement between Aug 8–10. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Mohsen Rezaei was appointed Secretary of Iran's Supreme National Security Council on Aug 9, confirmed by UPI and India TV News, during the second day of the Iran-Oman joint working group session in Muscat (Aug 9–11). Rezaei served as IRGC commander-in-chief from 1981 to 1997 and publicly characterized Hormuz choke-point control as "more important than dozens of atomic bombs." The SNSC secretary is the institutional layer that must ratify any Hormuz framework requiring IRGC operational sign-off. His appointment during an active WG session introduced a new procedural ratification variable not present at the session's start on Aug 9. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>

      <p>US President Trump stated Aug 9, in remarks carried by CNN live updates, that the United States is "low-keying" its negotiations with Iran and is prioritizing economic pressure over military action. No CENTCOM press release or authorized Pentagon briefing confirmed new US airstrikes on Iranian sovereign territory in the Aug 8–10 window. The Aug 1 pause on Iran-soil strikes, established following the June ceasefire-corridor agreement, remained intact through Aug 10 morning. With 24 hours remaining on its horizon, the strike probability fell to near-NO. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The Iran-Oman joint working group session in Muscat completed its second day (Aug 10) with one full working day remaining (Aug 11) before the Aug 12–13 post-session publication window. Entering the session, Iran's documented position stood at 5–7% cargo-value fee vs. Oman's 3% cap, with Iran demanding control of both shipping lanes vs. Oman's proposed 50-50 split. Oman's Malacca-style voluntary-fee proposal, tabled July 28, was rejected by Iran before the current session opened. Bloomberg's Aug 6 "agreed in principle" report — the only positive basis entering this session — had not been corroborated by any official IRNA, ONA, or TIER1 source as of Aug 10 morning. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>Six consecutive Israeli opinion surveys published through Aug 7 placed the combined right-wing Knesset seat total at approximately 50–51 seats, ten below the 61-seat threshold required for a governing majority. Benjamin Netanyahu confirmed his candidacy on July 24; the October 27 election date was formally fixed by the July 17 Knesset dissolution vote. No new polling was published in the Aug 8–10 window. → supports <span class="cite">F6</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

