  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Press TV carried Araghchi's four-condition statement on Aug 9 under framing of "Iran's sovereign red lines" and "non-negotiable prerequisites." State media presented the conditions as clarifying rather than blocking Iran's diplomatic position — a framing that places maximum political cost on any leadership choice to quietly set aside the conditions through back channels. The IRNA readout omitted the war-damage compensation demand from its headline but included it in body text; Fars ran the compensation demand as the lead item, diverging on emphasis. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Tasnim and IRNA announced Rezaei's SNSC appointment on Aug 9 with no direct reference to the Muscat WG session, presenting the appointment as a routine institutional transition. State-media treatment gave the appointment zero explicit Hormuz framing; no editorial linked his new role to the ongoing Muscat negotiations. Fars and Kayhan did not carry the appointment as a top-line story on Aug 9. The institutional overlap between the SNSC secretary role and IRGC operational ratification of any Hormuz framework is structurally real regardless of the absence of official cross-referencing. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>

      <p>Kayhan's Aug 9 editorial characterized the Muscat WG session as a demonstration that "Iran administers the Strait, not rents it," a formulation rejecting any fee structure as a sovereign concession rather than a revenue mechanism. IRIB domestic coverage framed the session as Iran "hosting" maritime governance discussions, rather than negotiating access rights. Both editorial lines have appeared in Iranian state media in similar form since July 2026 and are not new departures from established framing. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

